    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d575">

                <pb facs="0613" n="513" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg513"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/7001: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-06-09T15:00:00+09:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 9, 1920—3
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 9—2:27 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>288. My May 25, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="45" xml:id="d575-f1"/> and June 4, 6 p.m. The
                    War Office yesterday issued statement reporting that the 100 Japanese residents
                    who survived the Nikolaevsk massacre in March were apparently <pb facs="0614"
                        n="514" xml:id="pg514"/> slaughtered about May 25th. Public feeling is
                    running high and demanding punitive measures against Partisans. There are also
                    suggestions in the press that northern Sakhalin should be occupied as a
                    guarantee for reparation to be exacted after a Russian government, which can be
                    dealt with, is established.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
